# Copy to deploy/.env and fill in every value. deploy/.env is gitignored.
#
# Generate the secrets:
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD        openssl rand -base64 32
#   JWT_SECRET               openssl rand -base64 48     (must be >= 32 chars)
#   REALTIME_ENC_KEY         openssl rand -hex 8         (EXACTLY 16 characters)
#   REALTIME_SECRET_KEY_BASE openssl rand -base64 48
#   OTP_PEPPER               openssl rand -base64 48     (>= 32 chars, different per environment)
#   PARTNER_SEAL_KEY         openssl rand -base64 48     (>= 32 chars, different per environment;
#                            OPTIONAL — without it a partner connection can verify an inbound push
#                            and cannot make an outbound pull. Its own secret, not a subkey of the
#                            pepper: rotating the pepper would otherwise force every partner token
#                            to be re-sealed.)
#
# ANON_KEY and SERVICE_ROLE_KEY are JWTs signed with JWT_SECRET. Mint them at
# https://supabase.com/docs/guides/self-hosting#api-keys (the generator runs in
# the browser; nothing leaves the page), or with any JWT tool using:
#   anon:         { "role": "anon",         "iss": "supabase", "iat": ..., "exp": ... }
#   service_role: { "role": "service_role", "iss": "supabase", "iat": ..., "exp": ... }
#
# SERVICE_ROLE_KEY bypasses every RLS policy. It belongs only on the server, in
# the edge functions. It must never reach the browser bundle.

# ── Core ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_EXPIRY=3600

ANON_KEY=
SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── URLs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PUBLIC_URL is where Kong is reachable — the value that becomes
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL in the frontend build.
PUBLIC_URL=https://api.your-domain.de
# APP_URL is where the SPA is served. Auth redirect links are built from it.
APP_URL=https://app.your-domain.de

# ── Mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required. GoTrue and the edge functions both send through here, and the
# functions send every one-time code: signup, "Passwort vergessen", invitations,
# email changes. Without it nobody can sign up and a locked-out user has no way
# back in. Port 465 is TLS from the first byte; any other port must offer
# STARTTLS, except staging's catcher `mailpit`. Production uses Resend on 2587
# (deploy/set-resend.sh).
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_SENDER=noreply@your-domain.de
SMTP_SENDER_NAME=El-Professor

# ── One-time codes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The HMAC key the functions hash codes with; the database never sees it.
# At least 32 characters, different in every environment, not quoted.
# deploy.sh refuses a release without it.
OTP_PEPPER=
# Seals the partner bearer token so an outbound pull can replay it. Leave
# empty until the Reidey connection is in use; nothing reads it before then.
PARTNER_SEAL_KEY=
# Where an outbound partner call may go. CONFIGURATION, never taken from a
# request: these functions share a Docker network with Reidey's database and
# ours, so a destination from a request body would be an SSRF into our own
# network. Validated by _shared/partner-endpoint.ts — https only, a public
# host, no credentials, port 443, and no path.
PARTNER_REIDEY_BASE_URL=https://reidey.de

# ── Google sign-in ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off unless GOOGLE_SIGNIN_ENABLED is exactly true; empty or false keeps it off,
# deploy.sh refuses any other value, and the auth container will not start GoTrue
# with one. Switching it on also opens GoTrue's own
# signup, which Kong keeps closed on /signup, /otp and /magiclink. The kill
# switch is false here plus a redeploy.
# The client's authorised redirect URI is PUBLIC_URL + /auth/v1/callback. Write
# the secret on the host, never in chat.
#
# Staging and production may share one Google OAuth client (owner decision G3,
# 17.09.2026), and then GOOGLE_CLIENT_SHARED=true must stand in both .env files.
# deploy.sh refuses the same GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in both without it, so sharing is
# a written decision and not an accident of editing; what that catches is the
# other environment's id pasted in here while no opt-in was written. The opt-in
# on one side only is refused as well. Empty or false means "not shared", and
# any other value is refused. It does not catch a wholesale copy of the other
# environment's .env once both carry the opt-in — PROJECT_NAME does that.
# Write both files in the same sitting: one side alone fails the other side's
# next release. What sharing means: staging runs a copy of production's
# accounts, so one client is one consent screen and one token audience across
# both environments.
GOOGLE_SIGNIN_ENABLED=false
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SHARED=false

# ── Passkey (WebAuthn) sign-in ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# SHIPS OFF, and cannot be switched on by editing this file alone. Off unless
# PASSKEY_SIGNIN_ENABLED is exactly true; empty or false keeps it off and
# deploy.sh refuses any other value. Beyond the shape of the values below,
# deploy.sh refuses the switch outright in two cases, and both are deliberate:
#
#   * on a supabase/gotrue older than v2.190.0, which does not read
#     GOTRUE_PASSKEY_ENABLED at all. The switch would be on here and off in the
#     product, every /auth/v1/passkeys/* route answering 404, with nothing
#     saying so. A GoTrue upgrade is its own release.
#
#   * while no migration admits `passkey` in the platform amr allow-list,
#     app_private.session_amr_permits_platform(). That is SATISFIED since
#     `20261027140000` (the owner's decision of 27.10.2026: a passkey is like a
#     password, for platform admins, for companies, for everything), so this
#     refusal no longer fires. It is kept, and documented, because the reason it
#     existed is worth knowing: before that migration the function required a
#     session's amr to name `password`, and a passkey sign-in records exactly
#     one method, `passkey`, on a new session — so turning this on would have
#     taken the whole admin panel away from every platform operator who used
#     one, with no error anywhere. If a later migration ever narrows that list
#     again, this guard fires again rather than the panel going quiet.
#
# Unlike the Google switch, this one does NOT open GoTrue's own signup: no
# passkey route can create an account (registration needs an authenticated
# session, authentication only resolves an existing user), so
# GOTRUE_DISABLE_SIGNUP and Kong's auth-v1-closed block are unaffected.
#
# ── The relying-party ID: DECIDED, and it CANNOT BE WALKED BACK ─────────────
# The owner took the apex-domain recommendation on 27.10.2026. The values to
# write on the host, identical in BOTH environments:
#
#     PASSKEY_RP_ID=elprofessor.eu
#     PASSKEY_RP_ORIGINS=https://elprofessor.eu,https://app.elprofessor.eu
#
# **Do not "tidy" this to a per-environment value.** Changing the RP ID
# invalidates every passkey already registered against it, so every user would
# have to enrol again — there is no migration for a WebAuthn credential. One RP
# ID at the apex covers production and staging with one credential, which is why
# both origins are listed: an origin's host must equal the RP ID or be a
# subdomain of it, because the RP ID has to be a registrable domain suffix of
# the origin's effective domain.
#
# This is the same shape as sharing one Google OAuth client across both
# environments (owner decision G3), **except that it is irreversible**. The cost
# accepted with it is the same: staging runs a copy of production's accounts, so
# one RP ID is one credential scope across both stacks. A per-environment split
# can always be narrowed *to* later by adding a second RP ID; an apex RP ID that
# turns out to be wrong cannot be walked back without every user re-enrolling.
# That asymmetry is the whole reason the apex was chosen rather than the split.
#
# PASSKEY_RP_ID is a bare domain — no scheme, no port, no path, no trailing dot.
# PASSKEY_RP_DISPLAY_NAME is what the browser shows while a passkey is created.
# Both are left blank here because this template is not an environment: the
# values above go in the host's own .env, where deploy.sh checks their shape.
PASSKEY_SIGNIN_ENABLED=false
PASSKEY_RP_ID=
PASSKEY_RP_DISPLAY_NAME=El-Professor
PASSKEY_RP_ORIGINS=
# Optional, all three at GoTrue's own defaults: how long a WebAuthn challenge
# lives, how many passkeys one account may register, and the per-client limit on
# the authentication /options route. Leave them alone unless something measured
# says otherwise.
PASSKEY_CHALLENGE_EXPIRY=5m
PASSKEY_MAX_PER_USER=10
RATE_LIMIT_PASSKEY=30

# ── GoTrue notification mails (all off) ─────────────────────────────────────
# GoTrue gained seven "something about your account changed" mails between
# v2.170.0 and v2.190.0. All seven are off, deploy.sh refuses any value but
# empty or false, and docker-compose.yml passes an explicit false besides.
#
# Why they are written out here instead of left to GoTrue's default: every one
# of them is default:"false" in v2.190.0's source, no production code assigns to
# them, and there is no master switch — so the upgrade itself sends zero new
# mail with this file untouched. That is a fact about one version. An upstream
# default flip is not the kind of change this project's changelog marks, and the
# cost of missing it is mail to real German fleet operators.
#
# Why not simply turn one on when it is wanted: these mails have no template of
# ours. This product's mail estate is German and byte-pinned
# (src/.../email-layout.test.ts pins five templates to their exact bytes), and
# GoTrue's stock wording is English. Turning one on is a slice that writes a
# German template into public/email-templates and points
# GOTRUE_MAILER_TEMPLATES_*_NOTIFICATION at it — not an edit here. Editing the
# guard in deploy.sh is deliberately part of that, so the decision is written
# down the way GOOGLE_CLIENT_SHARED is.
NOTIFY_PASSWORD_CHANGED=false
NOTIFY_EMAIL_CHANGED=false
NOTIFY_PHONE_CHANGED=false
NOTIFY_IDENTITY_LINKED=false
NOTIFY_IDENTITY_UNLINKED=false
NOTIFY_MFA_FACTOR_ENROLLED=false
NOTIFY_MFA_FACTOR_UNENROLLED=false

# ── Sb-Forwarded-For: do not set it ─────────────────────────────────────────
# GOTRUE_SECURITY_SB_FORWARDED_FOR_ENABLED (GoTrue v2.185.0) makes GoTrue key
# every rate limit on the `sb-forwarded-for` request header. The header is
# checked only for being parseable as an IP — no proxy allow-list, no shared
# secret, no trusted CIDR. Caddy overwrites X-Client-IP and nothing else, so
# this one arrives from the client untouched, and a caller that rotated it per
# request would defeat every GoTrue rate limit in the stack, /token included,
# and could forge the address in GoTrue's audit rows. It also supersedes
# GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_HEADER when on, rather than adding to it.
#
# There is no value for it here on purpose: it is a literal false in
# docker-compose.yml, and deploy.sh refuses a .env that sets it to anything but
# empty or false. If a future topology ever needs it, the proxy must strip the
# header first, and that is the change to make — not this line.

# ── Realtime ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXACTLY 16 characters. Realtime hands this string straight to
# :crypto.crypto_one_time(:aes_128_ecb, key, ...) and AES-128 takes a 16-byte
# key — the value is used as-is, not hex-decoded. A longer key is not a stronger
# key, it is a container that will not start: `Erlang error: ... Bad key size`.
REALTIME_ENC_KEY=
REALTIME_SECRET_KEY_BASE=

# ── Sharing the box ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This VPS also runs the ridy project. Kong binds to loopback on this port —
# change it if 8000 is already taken (check with: ss -tlnp | grep :8000).
# Whatever you set here, the reverse proxy in front must point at the same port.
KONG_BIND_PORT=8000

# Postgres memory ceiling. Capped so a Fahrtenbuch import cannot make the kernel
# OOM-kill the other project's database. Check real headroom with `free -h` and
# `docker stats` before raising it.
DB_MEM_LIMIT=4G

# ── Routing (optional, off by default) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The travel check of the Fahrtenanalyse can measure the road between two points
# with an OSRM container of our own (the `osrm` service in docker-compose.yml).
# It starts only when COMPOSE_PROFILES names osrm (production: osrm; staging:
# staging,osrm) AND its map has been built with deploy/osrm-build-data.sh, which
# is a long job and tens of gigabytes of memory for Germany and is never part of a
# release; deploy.sh refuses a release with the profile on and no build.
# The container's memory ceiling. About 9 GiB is what OSRM's own wiki scales to
# for Germany; check `docker stats` after the first week and move it.
#OSRM_MEM_LIMIT=12G

# ── Geocoder gateway (optional, off by default) ────────────────────────────
# The address locator of the Fahrtenanalyse asks the Nominatim that ridy runs on
# this host, through the `geocoder-gateway` service in docker-compose.yml (an nginx on
# this stack's network and on ridy's, the only door to their service). It starts only
# when COMPOSE_PROFILES names geocoder (production: osrm,geocoder; staging:
# staging,osrm,geocoder) and needs no variable: the name of their service and the
# budget are in deploy/geocoder-gateway.conf. Turning the locator itself on is the
# `travel_estimator` feature flag in the platform settings, not this file.
